{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794651749,"nonce":"RH36qDQQ1hFznTBvsv3zarO+aFw=","message":"Wire format and where the Haxe side stops (Haxe 4.3.6, the Haxe unit suite, 298616 nodes).\n\nPostcard works from Haxe. A 40-line writer (LEB128 for unsigned integers and lengths, zigzag then LEB128 for signed, one byte for u8, the variant index as LEB128 for an enum, fields in declaration order) wrote the typed AST of the unit suite for `struct Node { tag: u8, payload: Payload, kids: Vec\u003cNode\u003e }` and `enum Payload { None, Int(i32), Str(u32) }`. The Rust `postcard` crate decoded it with `take_from_bytes`: 8902 bodies, 298616 nodes, 0 bytes left over, 6.1 ms. The Haxe writer took about 565 ms for 1.21 MB (three runs: 567, 573, 563 ms).\n\nDesign for the hand-off:\n- The Rust types (serde derive, in a crate that owns the schema) are the source of truth. The Haxe writers are generated from them, so the two sides cannot drift. I did not check for an existing Haxe generator; I believe serde-generate has no Haxe target, so we would write a small one over a serde-reflection registry. The primitives stay hand-written.\n- The file starts with a magic number, a format version and a schema hash. The Rust reader refuses a mismatch and says to update the exporter. CI exports a fixed set of programs through Haxe and decodes them in Rust (golden files), and regenerates the Haxe writers and diffs them.\n- Haxe `Int` is 32-bit, so the schema uses u32 and i32, and 64-bit constants travel as two words. Floats are 8 bytes little-endian (`writeDouble`). Strings are UTF-8 with a length prefix, interned in a table.\n- Alternatives: MessagePack has a Haxe library but is self-describing and Dynamic-based, so slower in eval and larger. Bincode with fixed-width integers is larger. rkyv, FlatBuffers and Cap'n Proto need offsets and alignment that a Haxe writer would have to build by hand.\n\nWhere the Haxe side stops: it writes what only the compiler can answer (type identity and interning, `Context.follow`, field, class and enum identity, inline and abstract resolution, the DCE set, positions) as a normalized, structured tree plus tables. Rust lowers the tree to AIR (SSA, cells, trap regions, verify) and runs the passes. Writing AIR itself from Haxe would put all of the lowering in the macro interpreter. Measured there: a Map and Array loop of 5M iterations takes 1337 ms in eval against 360-393 ms for the same code compiled to .hl and run by ash (3.5x), and interning a type for each of the 298616 nodes through `TypeTools.toString` takes 1561 ms (8553 distinct types). Rust runs the same kind of work several times faster than ash, and AIR's invariants, verifier and tests live in Rust.","files":null}]}